Sure! Here's a simple MATLAB code that simulates a basic model of natural selection:

```matlab
% Define the initial population
population = [0.7 0.3]; % Frequency of the advantageous trait
generations = 100; % Number of generations

% Simulate natural selection
for i = 1:generations
    % Calculate fitness based on the advantageous trait
    fitness = population(1) * 1.5; % Advantageous trait increases fitness by 50%

    % Generate offspring using roulette wheel selection
    offspring = zeros(size(population));
    for j = 1:length(population)
        prob = fitness(j) / sum(fitness);
        offspring(j) = population(j) * prob;
    end

    % Update the population for the next generation
    population = offspring;
end

% Plot the frequency of the advantageous trait over time
plot(generations, population);
xlabel('Generation');
ylabel('Frequency of Advantageous Trait');
title('Natural Selection Simulation');
```

This code simulates a population of individuals with an advantageous trait that increases their fitness. The population evolves over time through natural selection, with individuals with the advantageous trait becoming more frequent in the population. The plot shows the frequency of the advantageous trait over time, demonstrating how natural selection can lead to the evolution of adaptive traits.

To call a PowerShell script from Terraform and allow for Read-Host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform to execute the PowerShell script. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_host.ps1`) that prompts the user for input using `Read-Host`.
2. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), define a `local-exec` provisioner to execute the PowerShell script. Here's an example:
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File read_host.ps1"
  }
}
```
In this example, we're creating an AWS instance and using the `local-exec` provisioner to execute the `read_host.ps1` PowerShell script. The `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass` flag is used to bypass the execution policy check, as we're executing the script locally on the Terraform provider machine.

When you run `terraform apply`, the PowerShell script will be executed and the user will be prompted for input. The output of the script will be printed to the console.

